JPL CODEC — Project Bio (GitHub Ready)

Project Name

JPL Codec


---

Short Description (GitHub repo tagline)

A reversible graph-based intermediate representation system for encoding, compressing, and comparing structured cognition across language models.


---

Long Description (README Introduction)

JPL Codec is an experimental structured representation and transformation system designed to convert natural language into a deterministic, graph-based intermediate format.

It functions as a bidirectional codec:

Encode: Unstructured language → structured causal graph (JPL IR)

Decode: JPL IR → reconstructed structure (or constrained textual form)


The system is designed for cross-model structural benchmarking, enabling consistent comparison of reasoning, compression fidelity, and causal integrity across different AI architectures.


---

Core Purpose

JPL Codec explores whether complex reasoning can be:

normalized into a shared structural format

preserved under compression

reconstructed consistently across different models

evaluated using deterministic graph metrics


It treats language models as lossy compilers of latent structure, and attempts to standardize the intermediate representation layer between them.


---

Core Components

1. JPL IR (Intermediate Representation)

A graph-based structure composed of:

Nodes: entities, events, systems, or states

Edges: directed causal or functional relationships

Ξ Layers: temporal or hierarchical execution strata



---

2. Compression System

λ (Lambda): reusable subgraph definitions

∅ (Pointer tokens): structural references to avoid duplication

Goal: reduce redundancy while preserving causal integrity



---

3. Transport Format (TCF)

A strict serialization layer for model ingestion:

linear, parseable structure

whitespace-invariant design

optimized for cross-model reproducibility



---

4. Evaluation Metrics (JPL Score Suite)

Planned metrics include:

Δ1: semantic fidelity

Δ2: structural graph integrity

Δ3: cross-model consistency

Δ4: grammar invariance under compression



---

Research Objectives

JPL Codec is designed to investigate:

cross-model reasoning consistency under identical structural constraints

emergence of shared latent graph interpretations

compression limits of causal representations

transformation stability under grammar locking

whether reasoning can be standardized as an intermediate representation layer



---

Key Hypothesis

> If cognition is structurally representable, then different language models should converge toward similar graph reconstructions under identical constraints.




